Fixed the one thing the evaluator flagged.

What changed: only CLAUDE.md's commands_spawn.rs bullet (the code half was already correct and left untouched).

The old sentence — "enforced default confinement, NOT a sandbox: relative paths and bare git operate in the worktree by default" — described one mechanism doing two jobs. It's now split into the two mechanisms that actually exist, with claims I traced rather than assumed:

• bash follows the worktree by cwd (bash_cwd → `StreamingBashTool::with_cwd`) — a relative path really does resolve inside it.
• The file tools (read_file/write_file/edit_file/list_files/search) take no cwd, so they still resolve relative paths against the process cwd. `dir_restrictions doesn't redirect them — it makes them **refuse** anything outside the worktree. I checked config.rs::check_path (resolves the path, then requires it be under an allow entry) to confirm the refusal wording is what actually happens, and tools.rs:1003/1015/1061/1091/1101 to confirm all five go through maybe_guard with dir_restrictions`, i.e. the change isn't inert.
• The pre-Day-163 defect is stated explicitly (write_file("src/foo.rs") hit the main repo while bash hit the worktree), plus spawn_dir_restrictions' None-passthrough / Some-widen-and-dedup semantics.
• The non-sandbox caveat is now scoped honestly: the guard covers the file tools **only, never bash**, so bash with a non-git absolute path can still write outside; the git-redirection class stays blocked by safety::detect_git_redirection_escape.

